I checked the repository first: it is an empty skeleton (`src/index.js` exports nothing, the test file is a placeholder), set up for Node with `node --test`, and the README says "No package downloads are needed." There is no spreadsheet or reference to one in it, so the questions about the sheet itself can only come from you.

Your three open decisions (where it runs, who can edit, old reports) all hang off the answers below, so they come in round 2.

## Round 1

❓ **Q1** - **Audience**: Who opens this app? Is it only your team and internal stakeholders such as managers and neighbouring teams, with nobody outside the company?

➡️ Yes: internal readers only. That makes the data internal-only, which decides hosting and login in the next round.

---

❓ **Q2** - **Where the spreadsheet lives**: Is the spreadsheet a Google Sheet? The alternatives are (b) Excel in SharePoint/OneDrive, (c) an `.xlsx` or `.csv` file on a shared drive, or (d) something else.

➡️ Google Sheet is my guess, not a recommendation; tell me which one it is. This determines how the app reads the data, what credentials it needs, and where it can run.

---

❓ **Q3** - **How weeks are stored today**: Does each week get its own tab in one workbook? The alternatives are (b) one long table with a week/date column, (c) a new file per week, or (d) one sheet overwritten each week, so old reports no longer exist.

➡️ One tab per week is again a guess. The "old reports" decision depends on whether the history exists and in what form.

---

❓ **Q4** - **Source of truth**: Should the spreadsheet stay the only place the report is edited, with the app strictly read-only?

➡️ Yes. You said the app "reads the same data", and read-only means no write-back, no conflict handling and no edit permissions to build. "Who can edit" then becomes "whoever can already edit the spreadsheet." If you say no, the next round covers who edits, where edits are saved, and what happens when the sheet and app disagree.

---

❓ **Q5** - **Stack and the no-downloads rule**: Should I build it in Node using only built-in modules, treating the README's "No package downloads are needed" as a hard rule?

➡️ Yes. The repo is already set up for Node, and built-ins cover an HTTP server, fetching and tests. If Q2 turns out to be an `.xlsx` file, parsing it without a library is real work, so we would revisit this.

---

Still to come once these are settled: how the app reads the sheet and how fresh the data is, where it runs and how people sign in, what happens to old reports, and what the report page shows.

