answer:
I looked at the repo: it is an empty scaffold (`src/index.js` exports nothing, one placeholder test, `node --test` as the test script, no dependencies, Python also available). Nothing in it constrains the design yet, so the open decisions are all yours.

Your three undecided items (where it runs, who can edit, old reports) all depend on earlier answers, so this round covers what they rest on. Answer "yes" to accept a recommendation.

---

❓ **Q1** - **What the app is for**: What is the spreadsheet failing at today? The app could be (a) an easier-to-read view of the same report for people who don't want to open the sheet, (b) a replacement for the sheet as the place the report lives, or (c) something that adds value the sheet lacks, such as summaries, trends or alerts. Is it (a)?

➡️ Yes, (a): a presentation layer only. It is the smallest thing that matches "reads the same data and shows the report", and (c) can be added later.

---

❓ **Q2** - **Where editing happens**: Should the spreadsheet stay the only place the report is written, with the app strictly read-only?

➡️ Yes. That settles "who can edit" with no new work: whoever can edit the sheet today. Editing in the app would add logins, write-back to the sheet and conflict handling, which turns a small app into a medium one.

---

❓ **Q3** - **What kind of spreadsheet**: Is it a Google Sheet the team edits online? The alternatives are an Excel file in SharePoint/OneDrive, or a file passed around or kept on a shared drive.

➡️ I am assuming Google Sheets; correct me if not. This decides how the app reads the data and what credentials it needs, so I can't settle hosting without it.

---

❓ **Q4** - **How the sheet holds past weeks**: Does each week get its own tab, with old tabs left in place? The alternatives are one long table with a week/date column, a single sheet overwritten each week (history lost or only in version history), or a new file per week.

➡️ I am assuming one tab per week; tell me the real layout. If past weeks are already in the sheet, "old reports" becomes a display question; if they are overwritten, the app would need its own storage to keep them.

---

❓ **Q5** - **Who can view it**: Should the viewers be exactly the people who can open the spreadsheet today, with nothing public?

➡️ Yes. Status reports usually contain internal detail, so the default should be no wider than the sheet's current audience. If managers or other teams outside that group need it, say who.

---

❓ **Q6** - **How fresh it must be**: Is it fine for the app to lag the sheet by a few minutes, rather than updating live as people type?

➡️ Yes. A weekly report doesn't need real-time updates, and a short cache keeps the app simple and within any spreadsheet API limits.

---

Waiting for the next round, because they depend on these answers:

- **Where it runs** depends on Q3 and Q5.
- **What happens to old reports** depends on Q4.
- **Node or Python, and whether to stay dependency-free** depends on Q3.
- **Which fields and layout the report page shows** depends on Q1 and Q4.

