I looked at the repository first: it is an empty skeleton (`src/index.js` exports nothing, one placeholder test, `node --test` wired up, Node and Python both available). There is no spreadsheet or sample data in it, so nothing constrains the design yet.

Here is round 1. Each question is worded so "yes" accepts my recommendation.

❓ **Q1** - **What the app is for**: What does the app give people that opening the spreadsheet does not? This decides almost everything else. Is the goal a clean, read-only page of the current week's report that people can open with one link, with no new analysis, charts or workflow?

➡️ Yes. Treat it as a presentation layer only; if you want charts, roll-ups or notifications, name them now so they are in scope from the start.

---

❓ **Q2** - **Who reads it**: Is the audience just your team and the people who already receive the report today, all inside your organisation?

➡️ Yes. An internal-only audience keeps hosting and login simple; anyone outside the organisation changes both.

---

❓ **Q3** - **Where the spreadsheet lives**: I can't see this from here, so I'm guessing. Is it a Google Sheet that the team edits in the browser, rather than an Excel file on SharePoint/OneDrive or a file on a shared drive?

➡️ Yes is my guess, not a recommendation. Tell me which it actually is, because it decides how the app reads the data.

---

❓ **Q4** - **A sample to work from**: Can you put an export of the spreadsheet in the repository (say `samples/`, with anything sensitive redacted) and include at least two weeks of data? Then I can read the layout myself, including how past weeks are stored, instead of quizzing you about columns and tabs.

➡️ Yes. This settles the data shape from the real thing instead of from memory.

---

❓ **Q5** - **Who can edit**: Should the spreadsheet stay the only place the report is edited, with the app strictly read-only? That would make "who can edit" simply "whoever can edit the spreadsheet today", with no editing permissions to build in the app.

➡️ Yes. Editing in two places means sync conflicts and a second permission system, which is a much bigger app than the one you described.

---

❓ **Q6** - **Old reports**: Should people be able to browse past weeks in the app, each with its own stable link, rather than seeing only the current week?

➡️ Yes. "What did we say three weeks ago" is the thing a spreadsheet does badly. How the app gets the history (reading it from the sheet or keeping its own snapshots) depends on Q4, so that comes next round.

---

These wait for your answers:
- **Where it runs** and **who can log in** depend on Q2 and Q3.
- **Node or Python, and whether to stay dependency-free** depends on Q3.
- **How fresh the data must be** depends on Q1 and Q3.
- **How history is stored** depends on Q4 and Q6.

