I checked the repository first: it is an empty skeleton (`src/index.js` exports nothing, one placeholder test, Node and Python both available, no dependencies). Nothing in the code constrains the plan yet, so everything is still open.

Your three open decisions (where it runs, who can edit, what happens to old reports) all depend on more basic facts, so this first round covers those.

❓ **Q1** - **Reader and purpose**: Who opens this app, and what does it give them that opening the spreadsheet doesn't? My assumption is that the readers are people who don't edit the report (managers, neighbouring teams), and that the app shows the same content as a clean page with nothing added. Is that right?

➡️ Yes: a read-only audience outside the editing group, same content, better presentation. If the real aim is something else (charts, trends across weeks, a link to share), say so now, because it changes most of what follows.

---

❓ **Q2** - **Where edits happen**: Should the spreadsheet stay the only place the report is edited, with the app never writing back?

➡️ Yes. That settles "who can edit" as whoever can edit the spreadsheet today, and the app needs no edit permissions, forms, or conflict handling. Editing in the app would be a much larger project.

---

❓ **Q3** - **Where the spreadsheet lives**: Is it a Google Sheet, as opposed to an Excel file in SharePoint/OneDrive or a file on a shared drive?

➡️ I'm assuming Google Sheets. This decides how the app reads the data, what credentials it needs, and whether it can read live or needs an export step.

---

❓ **Q4** - **Sheet structure and past weeks**: Can you put an export of the workbook in the repo (for example `samples/`, with sensitive values replaced) so I can read the structure myself? And when a new week starts today, does last week's report stay in the file, as its own tab or as earlier rows?

➡️ Yes to the sample, and I'm assuming past weeks stay in the same file. If the sheet is overwritten each week, the app would have to keep its own history, which means storage and a snapshot schedule.

---

❓ **Q5** - **Sensitivity**: Should the report be treated as internal-only, so anyone in the company may read it but it must never be reachable from the public internet without a login?

➡️ Yes, internal-only. This rules out public static hosting. If some content is restricted to the team itself (names, customer details, personnel matters), tell me and reader access gets its own design.

---

These wait for the next round because they depend on the answers above:

- **Where it runs** depends on Q1, Q3 and Q5.
- **Reader login** depends on Q5.
- **What happens to old reports** depends on Q4.
- **Live read or periodic sync** depends on Q3.
- **Node or Python, and whether to stay dependency-free** depends on Q3.
- **Page layout** depends on Q1 and the sample from Q4.

