I checked the repository first: it is an empty scaffold (`src/index.js` exports nothing, `test/example.test.js` is a placeholder), with Node and Python both available and a README saying no package downloads are needed. Nothing in it constrains the design yet, so these six questions come first.

Your three open decisions (where it runs, who can edit, old reports) each depend on answers below. Hosting, stack and data freshness wait for the next round.

❓ **Q1** - **Purpose**: Is the point of the app to give people a clean page for reading the weekly status without opening the spreadsheet, rather than to replace the spreadsheet or add new workflow such as approvals, comments or notifications?

➡️ Yes. A reader-only page is the smallest thing that matches "reads the same data and shows the report", and anything more changes almost every later decision.

---

❓ **Q2** - **Spreadsheet type**: Is the spreadsheet a Google Sheet?

➡️ I'm assuming yes. If not, tell me what it is: Excel on SharePoint/OneDrive, a file on a shared drive, or something else. This decides how the app can read it and what credentials it needs.

---

❓ **Q3** - **Editing**: Should the spreadsheet stay the only place the report is edited, with the app strictly read-only?

➡️ Yes. This settles "who can edit" as whoever can already edit the sheet. The app then needs no write access, no edit permissions of its own and no conflict handling between two editors of the same data.

---

❓ **Q4** - **Audience**: Is the audience limited to people inside your organisation (the team plus managers or stakeholders), with nothing publicly reachable?

➡️ Yes. Status reports usually carry names, dates and problems you would not publish. This answer drives where it can run and whether it needs a login.

---

❓ **Q5** - **How past weeks are kept today**: Does the spreadsheet already keep past weeks (for example a tab per week, or a dated block of rows) rather than being overwritten each week?

➡️ I'm assuming it keeps them. If it is overwritten, the app would have to store its own snapshots, which is a much bigger design.

---

❓ **Q6** - **Past weeks in the app**: Should readers be able to go back to earlier weeks in the app, not just see the current one?

➡️ Yes. Comparing against last week is most of the value of a status report. How far back and where the history is stored depend on Q5, so that comes next round.

