answer:
I checked the repository first: it is an empty skeleton (`src/index.js` exports nothing, the test file is a placeholder), with Node 22 and Python 3.9 available, tests wired to `node --test`, and no dependencies installed. There is no spreadsheet file in it, so the questions about the sheet below are ones only you can answer.

Your three open decisions (where it runs, who edits, old reports) each depend on something more basic, so round 1 covers those first.

## Round 1

❓ **Q1** - **Purpose**: What should the app do that the spreadsheet does not? My reading is that you want a clean, read-only page showing the weekly report, so people do not have to open the spreadsheet. It would add no new data, no new workflow, and no notifications. Is that right?

➡️ Yes. A page that only displays the report is the smallest thing that is useful, and anything more can be added once it is in use.

---

❓ **Q2** - **Where the spreadsheet lives**: Is it a Google Sheet, as opposed to an Excel file on SharePoint/OneDrive or a file on a shared drive?

➡️ I am assuming Google Sheets. If it is something else, tell me which, because this determines how the app reads the data and how fresh it can be.

---

❓ **Q3** - **Sheet layout**: Is the report one table with one row per item and a column for the week or date, so past weeks sit in the same tab? The alternatives are one tab per week, or a single tab that gets overwritten each week.

➡️ I am assuming one table with a week column. Pasting the header row and two sample rows (redacted if needed) would settle this faster than describing it.

---

❓ **Q4** - **Source of truth**: Should the spreadsheet stay the only place anyone edits, with the app strictly read-only?

➡️ Yes. This answers "who can edit" without building anything: whoever can edit the sheet today. The app then needs no edit forms, no conflict handling, and no permission system of its own.

---

❓ **Q5** - **Audience**: Will the viewers be only people inside your organisation, such as the team and its stakeholders, with nothing public?

➡️ Yes, internal only. If some viewers do not currently have access to the sheet, say so, because that changes how the app authenticates to the data.

---

Waiting for the next round:

- **Where it runs** depends on Q2 and Q5.
- **Old reports** depends on Q3 and Q4.
- **Node or Python, how fresh the data is, and whether it can be built with no dependencies** depend on Q2.

