I looked for facts first: this workspace is a stub (an empty `src/index.js`, no schema, migrations, models, or any reference to `users`). If you point me at the real repo or paste the `users` DDL, I'll look up the columns, foreign keys, and table size myself instead of asking you.

Round 1 covers the three decisions everything else hangs off.

❓ **Q1** - **What the split is for**: Is this a boundary cleanup, separating identity and auth data (credentials, login email, status) from descriptive person data (display name, avatar, bio, preferences), with no user-visible change? The alternative is that a product feature is the real driver, such as multiple profiles per login, team or organisation accounts, or handing profiles to another service.

➡️ Yes, treat it as a boundary cleanup with no behaviour change this sprint. If a feature is behind it, say so now: it changes the cardinality and which table keeps the existing ids, and that is much cheaper to design in than to retrofit.

---

❓ **Q2** - **Downtime tolerance**: Does the migration have to run with the app live, so old and new code both work against the database during the rollout?

➡️ Yes, plan it as expand/contract over several deploys: add the new tables, dual-write, backfill, switch reads, and drop the old columns last. Every step is then reversible until the final drop. A single migration in a maintenance window is only simpler if the table is small and a few minutes of downtime is acceptable.

---

❓ **Q3** - **Who else touches `users`**: Should we assume the app code is not the only reader or writer, and make an inventory of the others a prerequisite before the design is locked? I mean BI dashboards, ETL or warehouse sync, other services, admin scripts, cron jobs, and database views or triggers.

➡️ Yes, these are what usually break in a table split, because they don't show up in an app-code search and nobody on the sprint owns them. If the inventory comes back empty, it cost an hour.

---

Once these are settled, the next round covers cardinality and which table inherits `users.id`, the column-by-column assignment, and what "done" means by the end of the sprint.

